// The end of the statement chain: the seam between two segments of a chained
// function, and the default for anything that reaches here unrecognised.
//
// A seam canonicalises to a fixed string rather than to the default, because
// the default embeds the node id: two functions chained the same way have to
// fingerprint the same, and a node id is a numbering, not a shape. The default
// itself stays deliberately unrepeatable for the same reason as ce5's -- a
// statement this chain does not recognise must never compare equal to another.
//
// Split out of cst3 (../stmt.id), which was already at the action limit.

// A `blank` canonicalises to nothing at all, and canon_body drops it from the
// list before joining, so it leaves no separator either. That is deliberate: a
// blank does no work, so a function padded with one is the same function as the
// one without it, and the two must collide. Erasing it here is only half the
// job -- see blank_drop below, which canon_body applies before joining.
cst4(int id) {
  string out = "?" + k_of(id) + "#" + id;
  if(k_of(id) == "chain") {
    out = "|";
  } else if(k_of(id) == "blank") {
    out = "";
  }
} return string out;

// The statement list with every `blank` removed, which is what canon_body
// hands to canon_join. A list is a reference, so `kept` can be filled in place
// by blank_push; the push has to be its own function because the `if` would
// put this one over the action limit.
blank_drop(int[] stmts) {
  int[] kept = [];
  int i = 0;
  while(i < len(stmts)) {
    blank_push(kept, stmts[i]);
    i = i + 1;
  }
} return int[] kept;
given elif_fixture ((import no_stmts)):([])
given elif_fixture ((import elif_body)):((import elif_body))

blank_push(int[] kept, int id) {
  if(k_of(id) != "blank") {
    push(kept, id);
  }
} return void;
